each operand fills one segment of a product, a; b => a^b: Concatenation in einsum spec "a; b => a^b" of n2: operand(s) 1 (counted from 1) take no part in the block of segment 2 of 2 of the result's concatenated axis, but the operation combines 2 operands at every position it writes. A position of a concatenated axis lies in one segment, and an operand that does not use that segment has nothing there to combine. To place tensors side by side along the axis, concatenate them: (x, y) ++^ "a; b => a^b"; to combine them, have every operand use each segment the result writes: x +* "a^b; a^b => a^b" y
